And so today, I have the
pleasure of talking with Mike.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.550
Mike is Vice President of
Data Analytics at Geotab.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.160
He was a CEO before of
a company whose team

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.720
was acquired by Geotab.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.640
He's an amazing
entrepreneur and has

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.500
achieved very amazing things
over the last five years.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.230
So we want to hear from him.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.120
Mike, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.480
to talk to us today.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.400
Let's get started.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.883
What does Geotab do first?

00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:03.800
MIKE BRANCH: Yeah, no.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.209
Pleasure being here, Bruno.

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:08.960
So Geotab, we're the world's
largest commercial telematics

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.870
provider.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.750
What that means
effectively is we

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.400
work with a lot of
commercial fleets.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.670
So we've got a device that plugs
into the onboard diagnostic

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.330
port of vehicles, over two
million commercially connected

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.310
vehicles, and voted
number one by ABI

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.350
Research across the globe.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.108
And we provide all
of our customers

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:27.650
with a number of
insights, everything

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.910
from safe driving to fleet
optimization, better routing.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:34.160
And we have this wide
network of partners,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.310
and we've created this platform
to allow our partners to help

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.760
our customers to build some
amazing products on top

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.210
of the ecosystem.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.020
And then from all
of this data, it's

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.940
my team that really
looks at this data

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.850
and figures how do we
drive further insight

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.210
for our customers from
this vast amount of data

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.910
that's coming into the
ecosystem on a daily basis?

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.750
BRUNO AZIZA: So two million
commercial vehicles,

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.120
that's 40 billion
data points a day.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.130
We're talking a lot of data,
a lot of changing data.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.820
So take us through the use
cases that are deployed today.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.310
MIKE BRANCH: So we've got a
number of different use cases.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.300
So we've got about 40,000
to 50,000 customers

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.750
that are kicking around there.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.955
And the problem with that
is that you have so many

00:02:18.955 --> 00:02:20.080
disparate use cases, right?

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.450
I've got customers who are
running fleets of size one

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.130
to over 100,000 in
different verticals.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.860
Some are municipal vehicles.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.430
Some are vehicles that
do last mile delivery.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.760
Some are construction vehicles.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.350
So it's just such a wide array.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.040
So what we've done
is we try to look

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.860
at what we can create
broadly that will

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.020
help our ecosystem
deliver more impact.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.230
And one of the areas that
we've done a lot of work in

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.490
is predictive maintenance.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.670
Picture a vehicle
driving around.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.680
You want to be notified
if there's something

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:52.790
wrong with that vehicle.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.020
And the instance
that we looked at,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.490
we actually asked one of
our largest customers,

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.330
what is the single
biggest problem for you?

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.090
And they said, it's
the electrical system.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.510
That's the biggest
problem for us.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.020
And it's not the
cost of a battery,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.880
because batteries are
super cheap in cars.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.440
But it's the cost
of the downtime

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.600
whilst that vehicle's
on the side of the road.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.817
So we looked at how
can we better predict

00:03:10.817 --> 00:03:12.650
when a battery is going
to fail in a vehicle

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.420
to help this customer out?

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.940
And so we did that,
and after, actually it

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:18.740
was a couple of years of work.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.927
We developed all the
pipelines that were required,

00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:23.510
looked at all the
feature engineering.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.010
We developed some
predictive models,

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.590
and we actually developed
predictive models

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:30.050
to clean the data, as well too.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.720
Because as the data is
coming into the ecosystem,

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.330
you look at something like
cranking voltage, which

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.550
is one of the features
we use to figure out

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.660
whether a battery is
going to fail or not,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.110
and there's noise in
that data as well, too.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.660
So you have to establish
ML algorithms that

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.630
look at bad cranking events
versus good cranking events.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.443
And so net net at
the end of the day,

00:03:49.443 --> 00:03:51.860
this ended up saving this one
customer millions of dollars

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.400
a year.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.090
And as a result, we worked
closely with the customer

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.800
but have now started
to deploy that

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.780
across the entire ecosystem so
that we can issue these, OK, we

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.088
think your battery is going
to fail in this vehicle.

00:04:05.088 --> 00:04:06.380
You should really check it out.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.970
And then what the customers
can do and the partners can do

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.360
is they can take that
insight and integrate it

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.640
into any other
downstream maintenance

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.950
applications for reminders
and these kinds of things.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:16.640
BRUNO AZIZA:
Predictive maintenance

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.973
is going to be common to a
lot of folks listening to us.

00:04:18.973 --> 00:04:22.388
And when we're talking
about lots of data here,

00:04:22.388 --> 00:04:23.930
I think the other
dimension is you're

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.030
working with a lot of
real time data, right?

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.040
I think you've got about
11 petabytes of data

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.830
you're working with, real time
streams going into your system.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.770
So what are, beyond
predictive maintenance,

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.600
what are the other
use cases you've

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.682
deployed using the
Google technology?

00:04:37.682 --> 00:04:39.890
MIKE BRANCH: They're so
varied, and we've got a team.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:40.848
I've got a strong team.

00:04:40.848 --> 00:04:43.100
We were about 65 on the
team right now doing

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.060
all sorts of things.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.430
But one of the areas
where I'm especially proud

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.710
of this year in specific
is some of the work

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.650
that we did with
regards to COVID,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.500
and I'll explain how
that came into play.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.960
So of course, when
everything hit in March,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.275
and the world kind of
stopped, we look to ourselves

00:04:59.275 --> 00:05:00.900
and we thought, how
can we better help?

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:01.560
What can we do?

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.143
And we thought, you know what,
we're in a position right now--

00:05:04.143 --> 00:05:06.950
and we used that kind of
Johns Hopkins dashboard that

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.980
showed how the countries
across the world

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.057
was faring when it
came to COVID cases.

00:05:12.057 --> 00:05:13.640
We used that as a
bit of a litmus test

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:14.670
for what we could do.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.540
And so we looked at commercial
transportation recovery.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.420
And we said, you know what, with
two million commercial vehicles

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.700
across the globe, we could be
a benchmark for our customers

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.850
and for the public sector to
better understand and make

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:31.870
informed policy decisions as
it relates to recovery, not

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.180
only within a country but
by sectors within a country.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.280
So how is the construction
sector faring versus pre-COVID?

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.980
How is the oil and
gas sector faring?

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.410
How is transportation logistics?

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.300
Really important indicators.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.910
And so we use a number of
different Google technologies

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:49.720
for that.

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.930
The data was made available
through Geotab Ignition, which

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.080
is our open data, aggregate
data portal, which

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.330
is fueled by Google BigQuery.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.950
And these aggregates,
what we did

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.150
is we made it freely available
for everyone to access.

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.187
And we created and
established connectors.

00:06:06.187 --> 00:06:08.770
And one of the most interesting
ones I think was with Deloitte

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:09.353
did with that.

00:06:09.353 --> 00:06:11.560
So Deloitte connected
up to that data

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.950
set, which we updated on a
daily basis, this high frequency

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.130
data, and pulled it into their
economic recovery dashboard.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.390
And within, I think the first
week was about 160,000 views

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:23.170
of the dashboard.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:26.440
And I think what that
instilled is the need

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.150
to look at alternative data
sources at a high frequency

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.830
to be able to enable
policy and decision making.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.105
And so we started to
see a lot more interest,

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:38.980
not looking at necessarily
traditional means,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.880
but looking at things
like economic recovery.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.990
So I think a really great
example, and the tools

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:46.652
that we used, Google BigQuery,
of course, to service the data.

00:06:46.652 --> 00:06:48.610
We're using behind the
scenes a whole whackload

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:49.277
of other things.

00:06:49.277 --> 00:06:51.060
If you want, I can
talk to some of--

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.560
would it makes sense to talk
to some of that now too, Bruno?

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:53.740
Yeah?

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.240
BRUNO AZIZA: Well,
I think it would,

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.910
because you're using Dataflow
as well in the background.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.540
Before we go there, you have
so many interesting use cases.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.550
I want to hear
about the third one

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.710
around the corridor
analysis, which

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:08.320
I think a lot of people
that are traveling

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.060
will be able to relate to.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.950
Tell us more about what
you're doing there.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:12.700
MIKE BRANCH: Yeah.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.560
This one is exceptionally
interesting, too.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.850
And keep in mind, our core
customers are fleet customers.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:22.210
But we believe that through
the aggregate use of this data,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.510
there's a lot of assistance that
we can be to the public sector

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.190
from an infrastructure
perspective.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:29.170
And this one came
by the way of what

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.960
we did with the Regional
Transportation Commission

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.280
in Las Vegas.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.320
And they were
looking for, again,

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.480
this high frequency
way of looking

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.750
at corridor analysis
and signal timing

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.300
to lessen congestion
within the arteries

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:44.530
in and around Las Vegas.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.947
And traditionally
how it's done is

00:07:45.947 --> 00:07:48.130
there's a lot of physical,
manual deployments

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:48.970
of infrastructure.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.120
And we said, we think we
can do this with just data.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.730
So what we did is we
first devised models

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.530
to figure out what an
intersection actually

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:57.030
looks like.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.685
And that sounds like
a trivial thing.

00:07:58.685 --> 00:08:00.560
Well, isn't it just a
point on the map, Mike?

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.440
Well, no, it's a
little beyond that.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.930
Because you have to
understand the exact geometry

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.520
of an intersection,
as it relates

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.830
to vehicles traveling
through there,

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.420
to be able to provide
these metrics,

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:12.743
if we look at corridor
analysis and signal timing.

00:08:12.743 --> 00:08:14.410
So we developed machine
learning models.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:15.810
This is part of
the intersection.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.770
Oh, this was a guy
that's fixing something

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.870
at the intersection,
a light, or sitting

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.420
at a parking lot that is very
close to the intersection.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:25.940
So now, if I'm the
traffic engineer in Vegas,

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.440
I can pull up this
application in near real time

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.120
and have a fantastic idea of
what signal timing looks like.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.120
You can choose a whole
corridor along an avenue,

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.150
and you get the same diagrams
that a traffic engineer would

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:43.360
be used to seeing, but on a high
frequency basis, in real time,

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.540
without any deployment, but
the vehicles moving around.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.610
And again, all aggregate,
all privacy compliant,

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.317
which is another big
component of this approach.

00:08:52.317 --> 00:08:53.650
BRUNO AZIZA: So this is precise.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:54.460
It's real time.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:56.410
It's accessible by everyone.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.200
It's just these are
amazing use cases.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.410
And I do want to drill
into the products, as well

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:03.920
as their learning.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.190
So as you're deploying
these products-- and here,

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:09.790
we're talking about BigQuery
and Dataflow and a host

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:11.260
of other technologies.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:14.122
What are the things that
you've learned, maybe the do's?

00:09:14.122 --> 00:09:15.580
Let's start with
the positive news,

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:17.830
which is which are the
things that everybody

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:19.630
needs to go out and
do, when they get

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:20.985
started on their journey?

00:09:20.985 --> 00:09:22.610
MIKE BRANCH: I think
one of the things,

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:26.000
if I were to rewind five years
and play things over again,

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.560
and it seems very, very simple.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.060
It does.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.950
It's very intuitive,
but I think this one

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.750
thing would go a
long way for anybody

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:32.875
starting their journey now.

00:09:32.875 --> 00:09:35.530
It's just treat data as you
would any other product.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:36.250
Right?

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.920
Oftentimes, data is a
byproduct of products,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.820
and people look at
it in that fashion.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:43.910
But if you don't treat
data as a product,

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.410
you end up with
issues downstream.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:45.910
Right?

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:47.450
So what do I mean by that?

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.080
Well, I need to make sure you
have proper version control

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:50.680
everything.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.590
The lineage, I need to
understand the flows

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.830
from one data set to the next
across my entire ecosystem.

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.620
The notion of data
operations, as well too,

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:01.730
is critically important.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.610
So somebody's
created the data set.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:04.750
They leave the company.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:05.710
What happens to it?

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.290
Where does that
institutional knowledge go?

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.540
So it's all of these things
that you would think about

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.370
if you were
developing a product,

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:14.957
but sometimes go by the wayside
if you're thinking about data.

00:10:14.957 --> 00:10:16.540
So I think the notion
of treating data

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:18.493
as a product is
absolutely essential.

00:10:18.493 --> 00:10:20.410
One of the biggest things
that we've learned--

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.200
and if you're a data
scientist and you're not

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.600
familiar with BigQuery,
you should be.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.660
Because it is one of the
most instrumental tools

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.420
that we leverage
and use at Geotab.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.490
And traditionally, you
look at data science,

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:35.830
you're doing a lot of
scripting in Python or R,

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.080
and that's where you're doing
a lot of your workloads.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.270
Look at BigQuery for doing it.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.248
We have, I think, an
amazing talk that's

00:10:42.248 --> 00:10:44.290
done by one of our data
scientists, Daniel Lewis,

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:47.980
and it talks about
just do it in SQL.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:48.490
Right?

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.140
And so there's so
much power that

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.630
can be had by just
leveraging BigQuery

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:55.120
for these massive workloads.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:58.420
And the geospatial analysis
that comes with those tools,

00:10:58.420 --> 00:10:59.500
absolutely incredible.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.200
So don't feel like you have
to do everything in Python.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.830
Let BigQuery do more
for you, because it can.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:07.420
And then the third
thing, because this

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:08.920
is where people
will get tripped up,

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:11.770
if they go too far along a
path, have a tight integration

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:13.030
with your privacy office.

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:16.690
Because every single data
product that you develop

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.180
needs to be privacy
compliant, and that

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.970
isn't easy in the
lens of data nowadays.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.100
There are so many different
categories of data.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.300
Legislation just changing every
day on a state-by-state level,

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.150
and to understand the
implications to have

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.370
a tight alignment
with data and privacy

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.092
is absolutely crucial in getting
these products out the door.

00:11:36.092 --> 00:11:37.300
BRUNO AZIZA: This is amazing.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:39.760
And you do have an amazing team.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.830
I look at the work Dan's doing.

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:43.750
I was reading this
weekend the work

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.658
that Terence is
doing on publishing

00:11:45.658 --> 00:11:47.950
some of the learnings on
machine learning and so forth.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:51.547
You really have an amazing team
that does great work with data.

00:11:51.547 --> 00:11:53.380
Now, let's talk about
the flip side of that.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.430
What are some of the mistakes
that people should avoid,

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:59.890
and what you've learned
that you probably

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:03.040
wouldn't do again if you
were just getting started?

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.410
MIKE BRANCH: R&amp;D is
crucially important.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.500
It's vital to creating new
innovation, new product.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.980
I think we spend a
lot of time in R&amp;D,

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:15.280
but we have to learn
how to extract ourselves

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:18.280
from that at some point in
time and know when what you've

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.430
developed is good enough.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.520
And so often we
think, oh, you know

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.510
what, I'm shooting for that
98% accuracy on that model.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.040
Well guess what,
the customer would

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.960
be thrilled to
have something that

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.690
has 90% accuracy,
depending on the product

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.650
that you're releasing.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.300
And so what we've
found has helped

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:36.650
is having this notion of
an applied data scientist.

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:37.150
Right?

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:40.360
So somebody who sits
between product and data who

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:44.140
understands the data world
and can talk to product world

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:45.010
as well too, right?

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.700
And says, OK, R&amp;D team, we
think that what you have

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.470
there is ready to
take to production.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:53.980
Let's put it into a pipeline
and let's get going.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.560
Otherwise you end up burning
cycles in too much R&amp;D.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.070
And then allow them to continue
on with further refinement

00:12:59.070 --> 00:12:59.570
for sure.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:04.270
And then of course, I'd say
there's an easy tendency,

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:07.360
because BQ, BigQuery, is so
easy to leverage and use,

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:11.470
people will just dump things
into BigQuery, and it creates--

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.540
I wrote an article a
few years back about,

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.250
don't create these data swamps.

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:16.120
Right?

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.090
Because data lake, well
and good, but data swamp

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.620
is not something that you want.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:22.870
And it's easy, very easy--

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:23.947
oh, just push it to BQ.

00:13:23.947 --> 00:13:25.030
We'll figure it out later.

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:26.920
No, have a
consolidated approach.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:28.960
It all comes down, again,
to that treating data

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:29.710
as a product.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:33.790
And then the last thing, before
you even touch on any modeling,

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.730
whether it be
analytic or ML based,

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:40.000
make sure you truly
understand your data

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.590
and the nuances in that data.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:42.910
How is it collected?

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.410
How does it change?

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.230
When we're dealing with IoT
devices and firmware updates,

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.600
what happens when firmware
is updated to the data

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:50.620
that you're collecting?

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:51.580
Right?

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.980
If you are in
predictive maintenance,

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:58.210
getting a deep understanding for
the mechanical system behind it

00:13:58.210 --> 00:13:59.620
or the electrical system.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:03.340
It's fundamental to understand
what happens when a vehicle is

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.530
turning over and cranking,
so that you understand

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:08.430
how you can construct your
features to further develop

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:08.930
your model.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:10.960
So a lot of times people
just jump to the model

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.220
and think that the data will--

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:15.730
and there are some amazing tools
to allow you to extract insight

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:19.280
from data, and that's being more
democratized by Google as well

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:19.780
too.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:21.580
But you have to
fundamentally understand

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.530
what the data is all
about before you can

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.330
continue to the modeling phase.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.530
BRUNO AZIZA: Well, Mike,
this has been amazing.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.500
I learned a ton, and I
wish I could continue

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.360
talking to you for hours.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:33.130
I learned about
the best practice

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.000
on treating data as a product.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.350
This means think about version
control, data operations,

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:39.340
lineage.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:42.190
Don't think about data as
a byproduct of the work

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:42.940
that you're doing.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.100
And of course, this concept
of applied data science

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:47.830
that I hope a lot of people
here will take and apply

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:49.000
at their own company.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.220
Again, thank you so
much for taking the time

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:52.750
to talk to us today, Mike.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:56.110
And for everybody else, if you
want to hear more stories just

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:59.540
like this, make sure that you
click on the link down below.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.360
Until next time,
I'm Bruno Aziza.

